---------------Describe Recurring User Jobs----------------------
Recurring jobs are scheduled to run repeatedly
The crond daemon reads multiple configuration files: one per user, and a set of system-wide files. 
The crontab command is never used to manage system jobs.
Using the crontab commands as the root user is not recommended due to the ability to exploit personal jobs that are configured to run as root. 
-----------------Example crontab Command----------------
crontab -l	= List the jobs for the current user.
crontab -r	= Remove all jobs for the current user.
crontab -e	= Edit jobs for the current user.
crontab filename	= Remove all jobs, and replace them with jobs that are read from filename. 
This command uses stdin input when no file is specified.
crontab -u = privileged user manage jobs for another user
-----------------------------------------------------------
Valid entries when writing recurring jobs:
a) Empty lines for ease of reading
b) Comments on lines that start with the number sign (#)
c) Environment variables with a NAME=value format, which affects all lines after the line where they are declared.

The fields in the crontab file appear in the following order:
1) Minutes
2) Hours
3) Day of month
4) Month
5) Day of week
6) Command

example: run a command on the 11th day of every month, and every Friday at 12:15 (24-hour format) 
15 12 11 * Fri command
1. A number to specify the number of minutes or hours, a date, or a day of the week. 
2. For days of the week, 0 equals Sunday, 1 equals Monday, 2 equals Tuesday, and so on. 7 also equals Sunday.
3. Use x-y for a range, which includes the x and y values.
4. Use x,y for lists. Lists might include ranges as well, for example, 5,10-13,17 in the Minutes column, for a job 
to run at 5, 10, 11, 12, 13, and 17 minutes past the hour.
5. The */x indicates an interval of x; for example, */7 in the Minutes column runs a job every seven minutes.
6. 3-letter English abbreviations are used for months or days of the week, for example, Jan, Feb, and Mon, Tue.
7. If the command contains an unescaped percentage sign (%), then that percentage sign is treated as a newline character, 
and everything after the percentage sign passes to the command as stdin input.
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-------------------------------Example-------------------------
0 9 3 2 * /usr/local/bin/yearly_backup    (every year)
0 = minute
9 = 9 am (24 hours)
3 = 3th day in month
2 = february
* = Every day of the week

*/5 9-16 * Jul 5 echo "Chime"
The following job sends an email that contains the Chime word to the owner of this job every five minutes between 
and including 09:00 and 16:00, but only on each Friday in July.

*/5 = every 5 minutes
9-16 = from 0900 to 1600
* = every weeks
Jul = july
5 = friday
The job starts executing at 09:00 with the last execution at 16:55

58 23 * * 1-5 /usr/local/bin/daily_report
58 = minutes
23 = 2300
* = every months
* = every weeks 
1-5 = monday-friday
(only monday-friday yearly)

0 9 * * 1-5 mutt -s "Checking in" developer@example.com % Hi there, just checking in.
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
--------------------------------------Recurring System Jobs-----------------------------------
1. System administrators often need to run recurring jobs. 
2. It is best to run these jobs from system accounts rather than from user accounts. 
3. Schedule these jobs with system-wide crontab files instead of with the crontab command. 
4. Job entries in the system-wide crontab files are similar to the users' crontab entries. 
5. The system-wide crontab files have an extra field before the command field to specify the user that runs the command.
6. The /etc/crontab file and other files in the /etc/cron.d/ directory define the recurring system jobs. 
7. Always create custom crontab files in the /etc/cron.d/ directory to schedule recurring system jobs. 
8. Place the custom crontab file in the /etc/cron.d directory to prevent a package update from overwriting the /etc/crontab file.
9. Packages that require recurring system jobs place their crontab files in the /etc/cron.d/ directory with the job entries. 
10. Administrators also use this location to group related jobs into a single file.
11. The crontab system also includes repositories for scripts to run every hour, day, week, and month. 
12. These repositories are placed in the /etc/cron.hourly/, /etc/cron.daily/, /etc/cron.weekly/, and /etc/cron.monthly/ directories.
13. These directories contain executable shell scripts, not crontab files.

SHELL=/bin/bash
PATH=/sbin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin
MAILTO=root

# For details see man 4 crontabs

# Example of job definition:
# .---------------- minute (0 - 59)
# |  .------------- hour (0 - 23)
# |  |  .---------- day of month (1 - 31)
# |  |  |  .------- month (1 - 12) OR jan,feb,mar,apr ...
# |  |  |  |  .---- day of week (0 - 6) (Sunday=0 or 7) OR sun,mon,tue,wed,thu,fri,sat
# |  |  |  |  |
# *  *  *  *  * user-name  command to be executed
--------------------------------------Recurring System Jobs-----------------------------------
-------------------------Run Periodic Commands with Anacron------------------------------
Anacron ensures that periodic jobs (daily, weekly, monthly) always run, 
even if the system was powered off or asleep when the job was originally scheduled.
The /etc/anacrontab file also contains environment variable declarations with the NAME=value syntax. 
The START_HOURS_RANGE variable specifies the time interval for the jobs to run. Jobs do not start outside this range. 
When a job does not run within this time interval on a particular day, then the job must wait until the next day for execution.
------------------------------------------
Main Configuration File: /etc/anacrontab
Timestamp Directory: /var/spool/anacron/ (tracks when a job last ran)
Execution Limit: Constrained by the START_HOURS_RANGE variable.
-----------------------------------------
The four required fields for an anacron job entry are:Period in days: 
The frequency of the execution (e.g., 1, 7, @daily).
Delay in minutes: The time the system waits before starting the job.
Job identifier: A unique name used for system logging.
Command: The actual script or command that will execute.
------------------------------------------
Systemd Timer
The systemd timer unit activates another unit of a different type (such as a service), whose unit name matches the timer unit name. The timer unit allows timer-based activation of other units. 
The systemd timer unit logs timer events in system journals for easier debugging.
------------------------------------------
Sample Timer Unit
The sysstat package provides the systemd timer unit, called the sysstat-collect.timer service, to collect system statistics every 10 minutes. 
The following output shows the contents of the /usr/lib/systemd/system/sysstat-collect.timer configuration file.
-------------------------------------------
After you change the timer unit configuration file, use the systemctl daemon-reload command to ensure that the systemd timer unit loads the changes.
[root@host ~]# systemctl daemon-reload
After reloading the systemd daemon configuration, use the systemctl command to activate the timer unit.
[root@host ~]# systemctl enable --now <unitname>.timer
